 QIP Components are Quick to run/make/maintain, Isolated from network/disk/dependencies, and Portable across browser/server/native.
 
-You write or vibe C/Zig, compile it to WebAssembly and have a deterministic puzzle piece that will run the same everywhere. These components can work with text, images, or any binary data, and then be composed into predictable recipes. Make a recipe you like? You can be confident it’ll work identically on mobile, in a browser, in your CI pipeline, on Windows, or whatever comes next.
+QIP is built around **Reproducible Rendering**: same input, same output, everywhere. A component is a self-contained WebAssembly module that renders from explicit input bytes, primitive uniforms, and, for interactive components, event/tick calls. It does not read the clock, locale, filesystem, package graph, environment variables, OS, device, chipset, or network unless a host deliberately makes something part of the contract.
 
-Because they are self-contained with no required dependencies they will keep working for years to come. This means you can worry less about supply-chain attacks, outdated libraries, remote-code-execution, and environment or file-system access.
+QIP is content-first: pass content in, get content out, and use MIME types to describe what is flowing through the system. It is webby in the same way HTTP is request -> response, but it is not tied to HTML, JavaScript, Custom Elements, props, imports, or app schemas.
 
-Your users get small `.wasm` modules that load fast, you get small amounts of code that is easy to review.
+Use QIP to add portable pockets of purity to existing applications. A component can render text, images, documents, archives, UI frames, or arbitrary bytes, then pipe into the next component when the workflow grows. If it works here, it works there. If it works today, it'll work tomorrow.
+
+Components are fast for you to create with coding agents and fast for users to run.
+
+QIP is **designed for agents to rip**. Big room to optimize. Small surface to attack. Components have enough scope for agents to generate and optimize meaningful code, while explicit contracts and quarantine keep changes reviewable and keep generated or third-party code away from filesystem, network, secrets, environment, and dependency access by default.
+
+Determinism makes cross-platform performance work safer. Every QIP pipeline stage is a benchmark boundary: feed it input, measure it, optimize it, and compare the output bytes. If the bytes still match and the stopwatch got faster, ship the same `.wasm` module everywhere that component runs. If it is fast today, it should remain fast tomorrow. Agents get enough scope to use Zig or C compilers, explicit memory layouts, and SIMD, while the Wasm sandbox keeps optimization inside portable code instead of host-specific GPU, filesystem, or platform side effects.
+
+QIP's goal is simple: if it renders today, it renders the same tomorrow.
 
